In case we have a function that accepts every object that satisfies the requirements of a certain interface,
if that function accepts a certain type of object as an input but we want to accept every instance of a class
that implements the interface I know what i wrote sound like poop salad of words so for example:

class anna {
void carry(Snowball ball){
    ball.attachTo();
    ....
    ball.unAttach();
    }
}

interface carriable (){
void attachTo (Child child);
void unAttach();
}

class FurFly implements Carriable{
void attachTo (Child child){...}
void unAttach(){...}
}


class Snowball implements Carriable{
void attachTo (Child child){...}
void unAttach(){...}
}

note the change we made, we had anna do all the things by herself she had carry and had the attack and unattach methods
after we made the interface carriable we note that the method carry in anna's class accepts only Snowball, so how do we change
it that it will accept any other class?
So lets make Carriable C (like template in C++ the whole point here is to make the object that those things could accept a general type)
so now we can do Carriable c = new FurFly();  this is allowed even that Carriable is a different type from FurFly
this is called casting! this works only since FurFly implements Carriable!
Since Carriable is the common factor to FurFly and Snowball we define them as a sub type compare to Carriable
so the casting from lower type to upper one is called UPCAST so Carriable c = new FurFly(); is an UPCAST
also carry(new FurFly()); that's an UPCAST as well
here's the trick, if we used an upcast and lets say Snowball has other methods as well, we wont be able to access them
since once we upcasted we can only use the methods of the interface! so take that in consideration!
we can also do DOWNCAST but its less useful and irrelevant for now.
